        <p class="profile-detail-region">Great work · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">785 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Begun on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>, founder of the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a>, and enlarged by his successors, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii">Abd al-Rahman II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> and al-Mansur; the Christian additions were made under Castilian kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, with architects such as Hernán Ruiz I, II and III</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A mosque with a hypostyle prayer hall of two-tiered arches on columns and a courtyard to the north, covering 180 by 130 metres after its expansions; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> nave and transept stand inside it, and the former minaret is encased in a 54-metre bell tower</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where it is the cathedral of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Córdoba and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the forest of two-tiered arches of red brick and white stone; the mihrab chamber and its Byzantine-influenced gold mosaics; the interlacing arches and ribbed domes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a>; the Courtyard of the Orange Trees; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> cathedral built inside the mosque; the minaret turned bell tower</span></div>
