    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Begun in 785 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>, the mosque was enlarged under his successors until al-Mansur's extension of 987–988, and its repeated two-tiered arches were kept in every Muslim-era expansion.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> in 1236 the mosque became a cathedral with only minor changes at first; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> nave and transept were inserted into its centre from 1523.</li><li>Its arches, gateways, minaret and domes served as models for later architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, and ownership of the building and the question of Muslim prayer in it remain disputed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The mihrab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> is the earliest known mihrab that consists of an actual room rather than just a niche in the wall, and its mosaics were made with the help of a master craftsman and about 1600 kg of tesserae sent by the Byzantine emperor.</p></aside>
    </section>

