    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">785</span><span class="rf-text">Construction of the Great Mosque began on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">958</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> completed the first true minaret and the reworked courtyard</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">965</span><span class="rf-text">The new mihrab and the domes of the maqsura were finished under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">987</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Mansur began the last great Muslim-era expansion, extending the mosque eastward</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1236</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and the mosque became a Catholic cathedral</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1523</span><span class="rf-text">Work began on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> cathedral nave and transept in the middle of the mosque</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1816</span><span class="rf-text">The original mihrab was uncovered from behind the former altar of the Chapel of San Pedro</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1984</span><span class="rf-text">The Mosque–Cathedral was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque.jpg" alt="Umayyad Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Mosque,_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Great Mosque was built in the context of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a> founded in 756. He was a fugitive and one of the last remaining members of the Umayyad royal family, which had ruled the first hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> until it was overthrown in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> of 750. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and, after securing support, took control of the Muslim administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> from its governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-al-fihri">Yusuf ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Fihri</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was already the provincial capital, and he kept it as the capital of his independent emirate.</p><p>According to traditional accounts, a Visigothic church dedicated to Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-of-saragossa">Vincent of Saragossa</a> stood on the site and was shared by Christians and Muslims until 785, when Abd al-Rahman I bought the Christian half for a hundred thousand dinars and demolished it. The story goes back to the 10th-century historian al-Razi and echoes accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a> in Damascus. Its historicity has been challenged because the archaeological evidence is scant, and archaeologists such as Pedro Marfil and Fernando Arce-Sainz disagree about it. A claim that the site held a Roman temple of Janus was dismissed by Robert Knapp as speculation.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba&#8221; cites where it covers Claims of earlier Roman temple, Visigothic church, Expansions of the mosque, Reconquista and conversion to cathedral (13th century):</p><ul><li>Robert C. Knapp, Roman Córdoba (University of California Press, 1983)</li><li>Jonathan Bloom, The Minbar from the Kutubiyya Mosque (The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York; Ediciones El Viso, S.A., Madrid; Ministère des Affaires Culturelles, Royaume du Maroc, 1998)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii.jpg" alt="al-Hakam II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlHakemII_dirhem_MedinaAzahara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numismática Pliego, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The original mosque had a roughly square plan measuring 74 or 79 metres a side, divided equally between a hypostyle prayer hall to the south and an open courtyard, the sahn, to the north. Its most famous innovation, kept in every later Muslim-era expansion, was the rows of two-tiered arches in the hall: horseshoe arches below and semicircular arches above, resting on columns, with voussoirs alternating between red brick and white stone. The rows divided the hall into 11 aisles, later 19. The original flat ceiling was of carved and painted planks and beams. The building was oriented more towards the south than the direction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, which reflected the street plan of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and a western tradition that the qibla lay between east and west.</p><p>The mihrab and maqsura added by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> lie at the south end of the hall. The maqsura, the area reserved for the ruler, was screened by interlacing multifoil arches and covered by three ribbed domes. The mihrab is a horseshoe arch opening into a small heptagonal chamber under a shell-shaped cupola, with gold and glass mosaics and Arabic inscriptions in Kufic script that include verses of the Qur'an. The courtyard, known today as the Patio de los Naranjos, was planted with trees as early as the 9th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i.jpg" alt="Abd al-Rahman I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_abd_al_rahman_i_21816.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numismática Pliego, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Construction began in 785–786 and finished a year later. The short period was aided by the reuse of Roman and Visigothic columns and capitals, about 120 of them in the original mosque. Syrian, Visigothic and Roman influences have been noted in the design, but the architect is not known. According to tradition and written sources <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a> involved himself heavily in the project, though how far he shaped the design is debated.</p><p>Expansions followed over two centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii">Abd al-Rahman II</a> lengthened the prayer hall by eight bays between 833 and 848, and new marble capitals were made for it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> built the first true minaret, 47 metres high, completed in 958 as an inscription records. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">Al-Hakam II</a> added twelve bays from 961, with the dome, the maqsura and the new mihrab, finished in November or December 965. According to Ibn 'Idhari he asked the Byzantine emperor for mosaicists, and the mosaic work was finished in late 970 or early 971. In 987–988 al-Mansur extended the building eastward by eight naves, covering 8600 square metres, because the river lay too close to the south.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii.jpg" alt="Abd al-Rahman III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_abd_al_rahman_iii_20386.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numismática Pliego, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Writers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> held the mosque to be the heart of the city, and one is quoted as saying that its beauty defied description. Scholars have read its decoration as a statement of authority. Jonathan Bloom suggests the minaret was partly a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>'s authority as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and may have been aimed at the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, which eschewed such structures. Nuha Khoury interprets the inscriptions around the mihrab as presenting the mosque as a universal Islamic shrine, like those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> as the instrument through which God built it. Some scholars see the mosaics as an effort to link the new Umayyad caliphate with the Umayyad caliphs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>After the caliphate fell, the mosque was looted and damaged in the civil conflict of about 1009 to 1030. Al-Mansur is said to have taken the bells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> and had them made into lamps for the mosque. In 1146 Alfonso VII briefly occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and according to Muslim sources the Christians plundered the mosque before leaving. When the Castilians took the building in 1236, the first Mass was held there on 29 June. The proposal in 1523 for a new nave was vigorously opposed by the city council. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> is reputed to have been displeased with the result and to have said that its builders had destroyed something unique in the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 1236 the structure underwent only minor changes: small chapels, Christian tombs and furnishings, and a cross on the minaret, which became a bell tower. Local Muslim craftsmen, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjars</a>, maintained the building, some of them as part of a labour tax on Muslim craftsmen, unique to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, that required two days of work a year on the cathedral. The Royal Chapel was finished in 1371 in a Mudéjar style, and a Gothic nave was made in the Villaviciosa Chapel in 1489. The great alteration began in 1523: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> nave and transept, designed by Hernán Ruiz I and completed in a Mannerist style by Juan de Ochoa, were built in the middle of the prayer hall, and the elliptical dome was raised between 1599 and 1607. The minaret was encased in a new bell tower, built from 1593 to 1617.</p><p>In 1816 the original mihrab was uncovered from behind the former altar of the Chapel of San Pedro. Restoration of the former mosque followed between 1879 and 1923, and in 1882 the building was declared a National Monument. Félix Hernández carried out excavations from 1931 to 1936. Scholars have noted that restorations since the 19th century have partly aimed at "re-islamicizing" parts of the building in architectural terms. The bell tower was restored from 1991 to 2014 and the transept and choir from 2006 to 2009.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-tlemcen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-tlemcen.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of Tlemcen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-tlemcen">Great Mosque of Tlemcen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_mosqu%C3%A9e_et_d%C3%A9pendance_Minaret_de_la_Mosqu%C3%A9e_003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BRAHIM DJELLOUL Mustapha, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Great Mosque of Córdoba is regarded as a high point of the architecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and one of the most important monuments of early Islamic architecture. It strongly influenced the later "Moorish" architecture of al-Andalus and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. The gate known today as the Puerta de San Esteban, with a horseshoe arch, alternating voussoirs and a rectangular alfiz, became a recurring motif in the region, and the minaret of 951–952 served as a model for later minarets. Georges Marçais traces later interlacing arches, polylobed arches and the sebka motif to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a>'s expansion, and its ribbed domes appear in simpler form in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-tlemcen">Great Mosque of Tlemcen</a>. The mosaics of the mihrab were admired but not copied.</p><p>The building is also a place of disputes. Muslims across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> have lobbied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> to allow them to pray in the building, with the Islamic Council of Spain lodging a formal request with the Vatican, and the Spanish church authorities and the Vatican have opposed it. The cathedral chapter registered the building in 2006, and defenders of ecclesiastical and of public ownership argue over who holds it. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a> visited in 1931–32 and wrote a poem, "The Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a>". Mass is celebrated in the cathedral daily.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba was begun in 785 as the Great Mosque of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> and enlarged until 988. It became a cathedral in 1236, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> nave was built inside it from 1523. Its arches and gateways shaped later architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</em></p>

